WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.880
A coffee filter’s job is pretty simple -

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:06.060
hold back the large, bitter grounds,

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.120
and let the liquid coffee through.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.240
Scientists have now invented a new technique

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.700
that flips the script, holding back small

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.880
particles but letting larger ones pass.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.840
Instead of sorting particles by size,

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.180
this reverse filter sorts them by kinetic energy --

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.420
larger, heavier objects with more force

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:24.900
break through the barrier,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.500
while lighter, slower objects don’t.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.920
Inspired by the process of phagocytosis --

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.660
when our cells swallow foreign objects --

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.420
the barrier itself is made of a self-healing liquid,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.640
similar to a cell membrane.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.120
To make the magical membrane,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.480
researchers need only a metal ring and two ingredients:

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:47.860
deionized water and sodium dodecyl sulfate (SDS),

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.440
a compound commonly used in laundry detergent.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.160
When researchers tested their filter,

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.780
they found it could be repeatedly penetrated without

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.400
rupturing, even after 3 hours of constant prodding.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.760
It could also hold back gas

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.940
while letting bigger objects through

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.000
and even kept the lid

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.080
on a beaker full of scrambling fruit flies.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.840
And by varying the concentration of SDS,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.340
scientists could finely tune the surface tension of the

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:15.660
membrane-- the key to its remarkable abilities.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.480
At very high levels of tension, only the larger or

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.860
faster-moving particles make it through

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.480
before the liquid seals together again.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:25.940
But at low levels of tension, smaller, or

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.820
slower-moving particles can do the same thing.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.880
That means scientists could use it in a variety of ways:

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.580
as a surgical membrane that blocks

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.040
out dust and germs,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.940
a barrier against infection-transmitting insects,

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.660
or even as a thin film that could

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.960
trap odors in a waterless toilet.

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.620
What’s more, the membrane is self-cleaning:

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.340
Small, trapped particles are easily transported

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.400
across its slanting surface and off to the side,

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.080
keeping the membrane free of contaminants.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.780
The resulting filter may make for

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.040
more successful surgeries and better odor eaters.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.080
But if you want the perfect cup of joe,

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.080
you might want to stick to an old-fashioned filter.

